## Method sources — cited, and quoted briefly

The reverse engineering follows published method rather than inventing one.
None of these works is reproduced here; `reverse/README.md` and
`reverse/relational-to-orm-reverse-engineering.md` cite them where each rule is
implemented, with short attributed quotations.

    L. J. Bird, "Data Reverse Engineering: from a Relational Database System to
      a 3-Dimensional Conceptual Schema", PhD thesis, University of Queensland,
      1997 — the rule numbering and Step 9's population analysis. The DREC
      schema of her Appendix F.2, transcribed as a test fixture
      (reverse/tests/scenarios/08-bird-drec-case-study.sql), is kept locally
      and not distributed; the scenario test skips it when absent.
    T. Halpin, K. Evans, P. Hallock, B. MacLean, "Database Modeling with
      Microsoft Visio for Enterprise Architects", 2003, ch. 8 — the reverse
      engineering chapter the rules are read against.
    L. Jiang, F. Naumann, "Holistic primary key and foreign key detection",
      Journal of Intelligent Information Systems 54:439-461, 2020.
    A. Rostin et al., "A Machine Learning Approach to Foreign Key Discovery",
      WebDB 2009.
    M. Zhang et al., "On Multi-Column Foreign Key Discovery", VLDB 2010 — the
      randomness test implemented as `_spread`.

The constraint verbalisation follows T. Halpin and M. Curland, "ORM 2
Constraint Verbalization", Technical Report ORM2-02, Neumont University, 2006;
the expected sentences in `model/tests/test_forml.py` are short examples
quoted from it, with the section each comes from.
